{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,
    "lib": ["ES2020", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true
  },
  // `e2e` IS TYPECHECKED, AND WAS NOT (FS-684). This read `["src"]`, so the six Playwright
  // specs and their setup were never seen by any compiler. That is how
  // `authenticated.spec.ts` referenced an `EMAIL` that nothing declared and kept doing it:
  // every run died with `ReferenceError` before its assertion, the file skips without a live
  // backend so no laptop showed it, and `vitest run` does not typecheck either. Restoring the
  // defect makes `tsc --noEmit` report `TS2304: Cannot find name 'EMAIL'` — one command, and
  // the whole class with it.
  //
  // `npx tsc --noEmit` is already a blocking step in `quality-gates.yml`, so widening the
  // include widens the gate; nothing else had to be wired.
  "include": ["src", "e2e"],
  "references": [{ "path": "./tsconfig.node.json" }]
}
